WEBVTT
TITLE Articulation Points Bridges And Blocks — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
A road network, one failure, two questions. Close a junction and see what happens; close a road and see what happens.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
Closing a junction removes the vertex and every road at it. Closing a road removes only that road, and both of its ends survive.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:24.000
So a junction and a road are different objects, and a map can be fragile against one kind of failure and robust against the other.

4
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
One depth-first search answers both, and also cuts the edges into the pieces that survive a failure. That is the plan.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.000
An articulation point is a vertex whose deletion raises the number of connected components. That is the definition, not a test.

6
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:46.000
A bridge is an edge whose deletion raises the component count. Both endpoints survive, so it is a weaker demand than the vertex version.

7
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.000
A block is a maximal set of edges any two of which lie on a common simple cycle. Loops are what tie edges together.

8
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Consequence: the blocks partition the edges, and a bridge is a block of exactly one edge.

9
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:06.000
The graph: twelve vertices, fifteen edges. Search it from J, a leaf, so that the root rule cannot be dodged.

10
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:14.000
The search produces eleven tree edges and four back edges. Fifteen edges, and no third kind.

11
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:22.000
There are no cross edges in an undirected search: an edge between two branches would have been taken, and the second branch would have become the first.

12
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:30.000
So the only way out of a subtree is a back edge climbing to an ancestor. One number per vertex can record how high it climbs.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:38.000
The discovery time is the order in which the search enters a vertex. J is 0, I is 1, and the chain runs down to K at 11.

14
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:48.000
low of a vertex is the smallest discovery time it can reach, using its own value, one back edge, and the low values of its children.

15
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Filled bottom-up: A climbs to C, so low of A is 7. G climbs to I, so low of G is 1. D takes the smallest of its three children.

16
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.000
A low value below the discovery time means the subtree escapes above the vertex. Equal or above means it does not.

17
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Take a non-root vertex u with a child v. If low of v is at least the discovery time of u, then u is an articulation point.

18
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:24.000
Why: deleting u removes every back edge that ended at u. The subtree of v then has no way up, so the graph splits there.

19
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
On the graph: D has three children. C gives 7 against 6, K gives 11 against 6, so D is cut off from two of them.

20
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Five articulation points: C, D, F, G and I. Not E, not H, not the leaves, and not the root.

21
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:44.000
For a tree edge from u to v, the edge is a bridge exactly when low of v is strictly greater than the discovery time of u.

22
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:52.000
The equality case is live on this graph: edge F to E has low of E equal to 4, the discovery time of F. Not a bridge.

23
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.000
But F is still an articulation point, because the same edge passes the weak test. Equality splits the two answers.

24
00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Where it is strictly greater, the edge is a bridge: C to D, because 7 is greater than 6.

25
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:10.000
The root here is J, with one child I. Low of I is 1, at least the discovery time of J, which is 0. The test says yes.

26
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:18.000
The test is wrong here. Delete J and the graph is still connected: J is not an articulation point at all.

27
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:24.000
Ask the direct question instead: the root is an articulation point exactly when it has at least two children.

28
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.000
The smallest graph where forgetting this goes wrong is a single edge: one child, and the test would flag the root.

29
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:36.000
Now the pieces. Keep a stack of edges: push each tree edge as the search takes it, and push each back edge as it finds one.

30
00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:46.000
When the search finishes a child v and low of v is at least the discovery time of u, pop down to the edge from u to v. That set is one block.

31
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:56.000
Seven pops, seven blocks: the triangle ABC, the bridge CD, the bridge DK, the four-vertex piece, the bridge FG, the triangle GHI, the bridge IJ.

32
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:04.000
Three, one, one, five, one, three, one: fifteen edges, and every one of them in exactly one block.

33
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:12.000
Draw one node per block and one per articulation point, and join a point to a block when the point lies in it. The block-cut tree.

34
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Twelve nodes and eleven edges: seven blocks plus five points, and it is a tree, as it must be for a connected graph.

35
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:28.000
A counting identity: the block sizes add up to the vertices, plus one extra copy for every block past the first that a vertex is in. Eighteen is twelve plus six.

36
00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:34.000
And the criterion: a vertex is an articulation point exactly when it lies in two or more blocks.

37
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:42.000
First belief: the vertex test can use the strict inequality. It fails first on four vertices: a pendant edge into a triangle.

38
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:50.000
Second: no bridge means no articulation point. The bowtie refutes it: two triangles sharing one vertex.

39
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:58.000
Third: every bridge has an articulation point at one end. A single edge refutes it: the edge is a bridge and neither end is a cut vertex.

40
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.000
Fourth: deleting an articulation point adds exactly one component. A star with three leaves adds two.

41
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:12.000
GATE CSE 2021, Set 1: four statements about articulation points and a DFS tree. One of them survives.

42
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:20.000
Three fail. All paths need not pass through u: four vertices suffice. A leaf is never a point: nothing found up to six vertices. A root can be one: three vertices.

43
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:28.000
The survivor is the root rule: the root is an articulation point if and only if it has two or more children. Verified on every graph to six vertices.

44
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.000
GATE CSE 2015, Set 2: a bridge cannot lie on a simple cycle. Checked directly: no bridge ever shared a block with another edge.

45
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:42.000
One search. Record the discovery time on the way in, compute the low value on the way out, classify each edge as tree or back.

46
00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:50.000
Then: weak test for vertices with the root rule, strict test for edges, pop the stack for blocks. Check one answer by deletion.

